WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.677 --> 00:00:03.488
- Stop doing the things
that you know are wrong.

00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:05.988
Make a damn schedule, and stick to it.

00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:08.234
Sometimes, y'know I can't sleep at night

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:09.715
because I'm thinking about something.

00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:11.989
And usually what I'll
do is go write it down.

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:13.977
It's important to
distinguish between those,

00:00:13.977 --> 00:00:14.810
because that's partly,

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.973
and this is back down to
the micro-routine analysis

00:00:16.973 --> 00:00:17.806
so it's like saying,

00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:19.696
well you're going to try and
make yourself more industrious.

00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:22.185
Okay, number one, specify your damn goals,

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:23.436
'cuz how are you going to hit something

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:24.269
if you don't know what it is.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.007
- Hello web nation, my
name is Evan Carmichael

00:00:27.007 --> 00:00:28.398
My one word is believe,

00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:30.375
and I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:32.647
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:33.958
So to help you on your journey

00:00:33.958 --> 00:00:36.133
today we're going to learn
from clinical psychologist

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.371
and professor Jordan B. Peterson,

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:41.467
and my take on his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.279
Rule number four is my personal favorite,

00:00:43.279 --> 00:00:46.233
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:47.885
And as always, as you're watching,

00:00:47.885 --> 00:00:49.834
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:49.834 --> 00:00:51.344
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:51.344 --> 00:00:53.659
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:55.404
You might win a prize as well.

00:00:55.404 --> 00:00:56.869
And also, when you write it down,

00:00:56.869 --> 00:00:59.610
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself as well.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:00.443
Enjoy.

00:01:01.536 --> 00:01:04.286
(dramatic music)

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:14.191
(swish)

00:01:14.191 --> 00:01:17.920
- Stop doing the things
that you know are wrong,

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.379
that you could stop doing.

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:22.597
Right, so it's a fairly limited attempt.

00:01:22.597 --> 00:01:25.692
First of all, we're not
going to say that you know

00:01:25.692 --> 00:01:27.311
what the good is or what the truth is,

00:01:27.311 --> 00:01:28.927
in any ultimate sense.

00:01:28.927 --> 00:01:31.124
But we will presume that there
are things that you're doing,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.225
that for one reason or the other,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.903
you know are not in your best interest.

00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:35.215
There's something about them,

00:01:35.215 --> 00:01:37.664
that you just know you should stop.

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.186
They're kind of self-evident to you.

00:01:39.186 --> 00:01:41.032
Other things you're going
to be doubtful about.

00:01:41.032 --> 00:01:45.073
You're not going to know which
way is up and which way is down.

00:01:45.073 --> 00:01:46.756
But there are things that you're doing

00:01:46.756 --> 00:01:48.445
that you know you shouldn't do.

00:01:48.445 --> 00:01:50.823
Now some of those you won't stop doing.

00:01:50.823 --> 00:01:52.555
For whatever reason, you
don't have the discipline

00:01:52.555 --> 00:01:54.285
or maybe there's a secondary payoff.

00:01:54.285 --> 00:01:55.876
Or you don't believe it's necessary.

00:01:55.876 --> 00:01:57.552
Or it's too much of a sacrifice.

00:01:57.552 --> 00:02:00.196
Or your angry or resentful or afraid.

00:02:00.196 --> 00:02:01.029
Who knows?

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:03.196
So forget about those for now.

00:02:03.196 --> 00:02:06.116
There's another subset
that you could stop doing.

00:02:06.116 --> 00:02:07.210
It might be a little thing.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.566
Well that's fine, stop doing it.

00:02:09.566 --> 00:02:11.039
See what happens.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.332
And what'll happen is,

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.784
your vision'll clear a little bit.

00:02:14.784 --> 00:02:16.961
And then something else will pop up

00:02:16.961 --> 00:02:18.956
in your field of apprehension

00:02:18.956 --> 00:02:21.102
that you will also know
you should stop doing,

00:02:21.102 --> 00:02:22.994
and that you could stop doing.

00:02:22.994 --> 00:02:24.425
'Cuz you've strengthened yourself a bit

00:02:24.425 --> 00:02:26.262
by stopping doing the
particular stupid thing

00:02:26.262 --> 00:02:27.495
that you were doing before.

00:02:27.495 --> 00:02:29.670
It just puts you together
a little bit more.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.529
And you could do that, repeatedly,

00:02:32.529 --> 00:02:36.000
for an indefinite period of time.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.663
That doesn't necessarily mean

00:02:37.663 --> 00:02:40.274
that you're going to ever
be able to formulate a clear

00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:43.828
and final picture of what
constitutes the truth

00:02:43.828 --> 00:02:44.919
and the good.

00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:46.642
But it does mean that you will be able to

00:02:46.642 --> 00:02:49.072
continually move away from what's untruth

00:02:49.072 --> 00:02:50.322
and what's bad.

00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:53.360
Y'know, that's not a bad start.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.859
Make a damn schedule, and stick to it.

00:02:55.859 --> 00:02:58.019
Okay, so what's the
rule with the schedule?

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:00.264
It's not a bloody prison.

00:03:00.264 --> 00:03:01.836
That's the first thing
that people do wrong.

00:03:01.836 --> 00:03:04.680
They say, well I don't
like to follow a schedule.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.943
Well its like, what kind of
schedule are you setting up?

00:03:06.943 --> 00:03:08.883
Well, I have to do this,
then I have to do this,

00:03:08.883 --> 00:03:10.689
then I have to do this.

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.361
And then I just go play video games

00:03:12.361 --> 00:03:14.444
because who wants to do all
these things that I have to do?

00:03:14.444 --> 00:03:16.224
It's like wrong.

00:03:16.224 --> 00:03:20.056
Set the damn schedule up so
you have the day you want.

00:03:20.056 --> 00:03:21.226
That's the trick.

00:03:21.226 --> 00:03:22.905
It's like, okay, I've got tomorrow.

00:03:22.905 --> 00:03:25.297
If I was going to set it up so
it was the best possible day

00:03:25.297 --> 00:03:26.380
I could have.

00:03:27.422 --> 00:03:29.338
Practically speaking.

00:03:29.338 --> 00:03:31.051
What would it look like?

00:03:31.051 --> 00:03:32.338
Well then you schedule that.

00:03:32.338 --> 00:03:34.312
And obviously, there's
a bit of responsibility

00:03:34.312 --> 00:03:35.507
that's going to go along with that.

00:03:35.507 --> 00:03:37.189
Because if you have any sense,

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:39.019
one of the things that
you're going to insist upon

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:40.820
is that at the end of the day

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.571
you're not in worse shape than you were

00:03:42.571 --> 00:03:44.877
at the beginning of the day.

00:03:44.877 --> 00:03:46.879
'Cuz that's a stupid day.

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:48.100
If you have a bunch of those in a row,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.257
you just dig yourself a hole

00:03:50.257 --> 00:03:51.586
and then you bury yourself in it.

00:03:51.586 --> 00:03:54.402
Sorry, that's just not a good strategy.

00:03:54.402 --> 00:03:55.873
It's a bad strategy.

00:03:55.873 --> 00:03:58.029
So, maybe 20 percent of your day

00:03:58.029 --> 00:03:59.930
has to be responsibility and obligation.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.351
Or maybe it's more than that,

00:04:01.351 --> 00:04:03.126
depending on how far behind you are.

00:04:03.126 --> 00:04:05.109
But even, that you can ask yourself,

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:07.608
okay, well I've got
these responsibilities,

00:04:07.608 --> 00:04:09.746
I have to schedule the damn things in,

00:04:09.746 --> 00:04:13.229
what's the right ratio of
responsibility to reward?

00:04:13.229 --> 00:04:14.582
And you can ask yourself that,

00:04:14.582 --> 00:04:15.415
just like you'd negotiate with someone

00:04:15.415 --> 00:04:16.844
who was working for you.

00:04:16.844 --> 00:04:18.953
It's like okay you've
got to work tomorrow.

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:20.870
Okay, so I want you to work tomorrow.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:23.617
And you might say, okay, well
what are you going to do for me

00:04:23.617 --> 00:04:26.103
that makes it likely
that I will work for you?

00:04:26.103 --> 00:04:27.698
Well, you could ask yourself that, y'know.

00:04:27.698 --> 00:04:31.311
So maybe you do an hour of responsibility

00:04:31.311 --> 00:04:33.069
and then you play a video
game for 15 minutes.

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:35.175
I don't know, whatever
turns your crank, man.

00:04:35.175 --> 00:04:38.166
But you have to negotiate with yourself,

00:04:38.166 --> 00:04:40.548
and not tyrannize yourself.

00:04:40.548 --> 00:04:43.140
Like you're negotiating with
someone that you care for,

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.303
that you would like to be
productive and have a good life.

00:04:46.303 --> 00:04:47.790
That's how you make the schedule.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.429
And then you look at
the day and you think,

00:04:49.429 --> 00:04:51.634
well if I had that day that would be good.

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:54.793
Great! Y'know, and you're
useless and horrible,

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:55.861
so you'll probably only hit it

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:57.491
with about 70 percent accuracy

00:04:57.491 --> 00:05:00.532
but that beats the hell out of zero.

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:03.149
And if you hit it even
with 50 percent accuracy,

00:05:03.149 --> 00:05:06.863
another rule is, well aim
for 51 percent the next week.

00:05:06.863 --> 00:05:09.820
Or 50 and a half percent, for god's sake.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.646
Because, you're going to hit that position

00:05:12.646 --> 00:05:14.777
where things start to loop back positively

00:05:14.777 --> 00:05:16.651
and spiral you upward.

00:05:16.651 --> 00:05:19.013
Sometimes, y'know, I can't sleep at night

00:05:19.013 --> 00:05:20.737
because I'm thinking about something.

00:05:20.737 --> 00:05:23.250
And usually what I'll
do is go write it down.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.394
I have some writing to do.

00:05:24.394 --> 00:05:26.282
So I get up and I go write
down what I'm thinking,

00:05:26.282 --> 00:05:27.491
and that usually does the trick.

00:05:27.491 --> 00:05:29.747
But because I've been
playing with YouTube,

00:05:29.747 --> 00:05:33.580
I thought, well, I'll
try making YouTube video

00:05:35.679 --> 00:05:37.867
and telling people what
I'm thinking about.

00:05:37.867 --> 00:05:42.034
And see if that performs the
same function as writing.

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:46.812
To me, the function of
writing, well it's two-fold.

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:50.032
One is conceivably, to
communicate with people.

00:05:50.032 --> 00:05:52.327
Although the fundamental purpose for me,

00:05:52.327 --> 00:05:55.160
is to clarify my thoughts, so that I know.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.759
Because if something is disturbing you,

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:03.043
what that means is that it
needs to be articulated.

00:06:03.043 --> 00:06:06.735
It's the emergence of
unexplored territory,

00:06:06.735 --> 00:06:08.015
something that disturbs you.

00:06:08.015 --> 00:06:09.795
That's the right way to think about it.

00:06:09.795 --> 00:06:12.972
It's unmapped territory
that's manifesting itself.

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:15.857
It's like a vista of
threat and possibility.

00:06:15.857 --> 00:06:18.604
And you need to articulate
a path through it.

00:06:18.604 --> 00:06:20.717
So that's what I was doing.

00:06:20.717 --> 00:06:21.948
It was like, I was thinking,

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:24.053
well this is bothering me,
and this seems to be why,

00:06:24.053 --> 00:06:25.823
and here's what I think is going on,

00:06:25.823 --> 00:06:27.894
and so I made the videos.

00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:31.758
In some sense I didn't
think anything more of it.

00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:33.991
You don't have to necessarily
have done anything wrong

00:06:33.991 --> 00:06:36.643
for things to get
completely out of control.

00:06:36.643 --> 00:06:38.422
It's a terrifying doctrine,

00:06:38.422 --> 00:06:40.422
but it's not a hopeless doctrine

00:06:40.422 --> 00:06:42.772
because it stills says
that there's a way forward.

00:06:42.772 --> 00:06:44.601
There's a pathway forward.

00:06:44.601 --> 00:06:47.181
And the pathway forward is
to adopt a mode of being

00:06:47.181 --> 00:06:48.953
that has some nobility.

00:06:48.953 --> 00:06:51.474
So that you can tolerate yourself,

00:06:51.474 --> 00:06:54.031
and perhaps even have
some respect for yourself,

00:06:54.031 --> 00:06:57.096
as someone who is capable of standing up

00:06:57.096 --> 00:07:00.016
in the face of that terrible
vulnerability and suffering.

00:07:00.016 --> 00:07:01.131
And that the pathway forward

00:07:01.131 --> 00:07:03.265
as far as the
existentialists are concerned

00:07:03.265 --> 00:07:06.848
is by, well certainly by
the avoidance of deceit,

00:07:06.848 --> 00:07:09.213
particularly in language,

00:07:09.213 --> 00:07:11.899
but also by the adoption of responsibility

00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:13.209
for the conditions of existence

00:07:13.209 --> 00:07:14.783
and some attempt on your part

00:07:14.783 --> 00:07:16.386
to actually rectify them.

00:07:16.386 --> 00:07:18.580
And the thing that's so
interesting about that is,

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.920
well two as far as I'm concerned.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.598
And some of this is from
clinical experience.

00:07:22.598 --> 00:07:24.141
You know, if you take people,

00:07:24.141 --> 00:07:24.998
and I've told you this,

00:07:24.998 --> 00:07:26.514
and you expose them voluntarily

00:07:26.514 --> 00:07:28.569
to things that they are avoiding,

00:07:28.569 --> 00:07:30.007
and are afraid of,

00:07:30.007 --> 00:07:31.299
that they know they need to overcome

00:07:31.299 --> 00:07:34.494
in order to meet their goals,
their self-defined goals.

00:07:34.494 --> 00:07:36.367
If you can teach people to stand up

00:07:36.367 --> 00:07:37.854
in the face of the things
that they're afraid of,

00:07:37.854 --> 00:07:39.354
they get stronger.

00:07:40.262 --> 00:07:42.282
And you don't know what the
upper limits to that are.

00:07:42.282 --> 00:07:43.472
Because you might ask yourself,

00:07:43.472 --> 00:07:45.010
if for ten years,

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:48.701
if you didn't avoid doing what
you knew you needed to do,

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.567
by your own definitions, right

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:52.617
within the value structure
that you've created

00:07:52.617 --> 00:07:54.237
to the degree that you've done that,

00:07:54.237 --> 00:07:56.154
what would you be like?

00:07:57.478 --> 00:07:59.712
Well, y'know there are remarkable people

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.438
who come into the world from time to time.

00:08:01.438 --> 00:08:02.987
And there are people who do find out

00:08:02.987 --> 00:08:05.182
over decades long periods

00:08:05.182 --> 00:08:08.434
what they could be like if
they were who they were.

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:13.102
If they said, if they
spoke their being forward.

00:08:13.102 --> 00:08:15.403
And they'd get stronger
and stronger and stronger,

00:08:15.403 --> 00:08:16.867
and we don't know the limits to that.

00:08:16.867 --> 00:08:18.559
We do not know the limits to that.

00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:20.006
And so you could say, well in part,

00:08:20.006 --> 00:08:21.963
perhaps the reason that
you're suffering unbearably

00:08:21.963 --> 00:08:24.251
can be left at your feet

00:08:24.251 --> 00:08:26.052
because you're not everything you could be

00:08:26.052 --> 00:08:27.289
and you know it.

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:29.409
And of course that's a
terrible thing to admit,

00:08:29.409 --> 00:08:31.300
and it's a terrible thing to consider,

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.387
but there's real promise in it, right?

00:08:33.387 --> 00:08:37.031
Because it means that,
perhaps there's another way

00:08:37.031 --> 00:08:38.082
that you could look at the world

00:08:38.082 --> 00:08:41.023
and another way you
could act in the world,

00:08:41.023 --> 00:08:42.599
so what it would reflect back to you

00:08:42.599 --> 00:08:45.196
would be much better than what
it reflects back to you now.

00:08:45.196 --> 00:08:47.183
My experience is with people

00:08:47.183 --> 00:08:48.689
that were probably running at about

00:08:48.689 --> 00:08:51.315
51 percent of our capacity.

00:08:51.315 --> 00:08:53.292
Something, maybe you can
think about this yourselves.

00:08:53.292 --> 00:08:57.682
I often ask undergraduates how
many hours a day you waste?

00:08:57.682 --> 00:08:58.996
Or how many hours a week you waste.

00:08:58.996 --> 00:09:00.270
And the classic answer is

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.959
something like four to six hours a day.

00:09:02.959 --> 00:09:04.873
Inefficient studying.

00:09:04.873 --> 00:09:06.857
Watching things on YouTube,

00:09:06.857 --> 00:09:08.539
that not only do you not want to watch,

00:09:08.539 --> 00:09:09.691
that you don't even care about,

00:09:09.691 --> 00:09:10.823
that make you feel horrible

00:09:10.823 --> 00:09:12.679
about watching after you're done.

00:09:12.679 --> 00:09:15.478
That's probably four hours right there.

00:09:15.478 --> 00:09:18.351
You think, well that's 20, 25 hours a week

00:09:18.351 --> 00:09:19.617
it's a hundred hours a month.

00:09:19.617 --> 00:09:21.626
That's two and a half full work weeks.

00:09:21.626 --> 00:09:24.562
It's half a year of work weeks per year.

00:09:24.562 --> 00:09:27.472
And if your time is
worth 20 dollars an hour,

00:09:27.472 --> 00:09:29.006
which is a radical underestimate,

00:09:29.006 --> 00:09:30.445
it's probably more like 50,

00:09:30.445 --> 00:09:32.993
if you think about it in
terms of deferred wages.

00:09:32.993 --> 00:09:35.201
If you're wasting 20 hours a week,

00:09:35.201 --> 00:09:37.817
you're wasting 50,000 dollars a year.

00:09:37.817 --> 00:09:39.531
And you are doing that right now.

00:09:39.531 --> 00:09:40.873
And it's because your young,

00:09:40.873 --> 00:09:43.795
wasting 50,000 dollars a year
is a way bigger catastrophe

00:09:43.795 --> 00:09:45.223
than it would be for me to waste it,

00:09:45.223 --> 00:09:47.483
because I'm not going
to last nearly as long.

00:09:47.483 --> 00:09:49.415
And so if your life isn't
everything it could be,

00:09:49.415 --> 00:09:50.250
you could ask yourself,

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.932
what would happen if
you just stopped wasting

00:09:52.932 --> 00:09:55.885
the opportunities that
are in front of you?

00:09:55.885 --> 00:09:58.466
You'd be, who knows how
much more efficient.

00:09:58.466 --> 00:10:00.311
10 times more efficient.

00:10:00.311 --> 00:10:02.166
20 times more efficient.

00:10:02.166 --> 00:10:03.927
That's the Pareto distribution.

00:10:03.927 --> 00:10:07.270
You have no idea how efficient,
efficient people get.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.353
It's completely, it's off the charts.

00:10:11.695 --> 00:10:14.480
Well and if we all got our
act together collectively,

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.435
and stopped making things worse.

00:10:16.435 --> 00:10:18.729
Because that's another thing
people do all the time.

00:10:18.729 --> 00:10:20.662
Not only do the not do what they should

00:10:20.662 --> 00:10:21.535
to make things better,

00:10:21.535 --> 00:10:23.967
they actively attempt
to make things worse,

00:10:23.967 --> 00:10:25.657
because they're spiteful, or resentful,

00:10:25.657 --> 00:10:28.080
or arrogant, or deceitful, or,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.964
or homicidal, or genocidal,
or all of those things,

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:36.747
all bundled together in an
absolutely pathological package.

00:10:36.747 --> 00:10:39.162
If people stopped really, really trying

00:10:39.162 --> 00:10:40.418
just to make things worse,

00:10:40.418 --> 00:10:42.612
we have no idea how much
better they would get

00:10:42.612 --> 00:10:44.570
just because of that.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:45.989
So there's this weird dynamic

00:10:45.989 --> 00:10:48.570
that's part of the
existential system of ideas

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:52.313
between human vulnerability,
social judgment,

00:10:52.313 --> 00:10:56.419
both of which are major
causes of suffering,

00:10:56.419 --> 00:10:59.212
and the failure of individuals
to adopt the responsibility

00:10:59.212 --> 00:11:01.764
that they know they should adopt.

00:11:01.764 --> 00:11:04.057
It isn't merely that your fate depends on

00:11:04.057 --> 00:11:05.753
whether or not you get your act together,

00:11:05.753 --> 00:11:09.062
and to what degree you
decide you're going to live

00:11:09.062 --> 00:11:11.089
out your own genuine being.

00:11:11.089 --> 00:11:12.874
It isn't only your fate,

00:11:12.874 --> 00:11:15.629
it's the fate of everyone
that you networked with.

00:11:15.629 --> 00:11:17.526
And so, y'know, you think, well

00:11:17.526 --> 00:11:20.827
there's 9 billion, 7
billion people in the world.

00:11:20.827 --> 00:11:23.150
We're going to peak at
about 9 billion, by the way,

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:24.940
and then it'll decline rapidly.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.242
But, 7 billion people in
the world, and who are you?

00:11:27.242 --> 00:11:30.318
You're just one little dust
mote among that 7 billion,

00:11:30.318 --> 00:11:32.680
and so it really doesn't
matter what you do or don't do.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.712
But that's simply not the case.

00:11:34.712 --> 00:11:36.175
It's the wrong model,

00:11:36.175 --> 00:11:38.739
because you're at the center of a network.

00:11:38.739 --> 00:11:40.548
You're a node in a network.

00:11:40.548 --> 00:11:41.702
Of course, that's even more true

00:11:41.702 --> 00:11:43.321
now that we have social media.

00:11:43.321 --> 00:11:45.632
You'll know a thousand people, at least,

00:11:45.632 --> 00:11:47.181
over the course of your life.

00:11:47.181 --> 00:11:48.966
And they'll know a thousand people each.

00:11:48.966 --> 00:11:51.446
And that puts you one
person away from a million.

00:11:51.446 --> 00:11:54.314
And two persons away from a billion.

00:11:54.314 --> 00:11:55.832
And so that's how you're connected.

00:11:55.832 --> 00:11:57.207
And the things you do,

00:11:57.207 --> 00:11:59.227
they're like dropping a stone in a pond,

00:11:59.227 --> 00:12:00.915
the ripples move outward.

00:12:00.915 --> 00:12:03.231
And they affect things
in ways that you can't

00:12:03.231 --> 00:12:04.457
fully comprehend.

00:12:04.457 --> 00:12:06.385
And it means that the things that you do,

00:12:06.385 --> 00:12:07.499
and that you don't do,

00:12:07.499 --> 00:12:11.261
are far more important than you think.

00:12:11.261 --> 00:12:12.499
And so if you act in it,

00:12:12.499 --> 00:12:14.388
when of course the
terror of realizing that

00:12:14.388 --> 00:12:16.994
is that it actually starts
to matter what you do.

00:12:16.994 --> 00:12:18.660
And you might say, well that's better than

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.519
living a meaningless existence.

00:12:20.519 --> 00:12:22.365
It's better for it to matter.

00:12:22.365 --> 00:12:23.970
But I mean, if you really asked yourself,

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:25.988
would you be so sure,
if you had the choice.

00:12:25.988 --> 00:12:29.120
I can live with no
responsibility whatsoever.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.130
The price I pay is that nothing matters.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:35.030
Or I can reverse it
and everything matters,

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:36.798
but I have to take the responsibility

00:12:36.798 --> 00:12:38.426
that's associated with that.

00:12:38.426 --> 00:12:39.622
It's not so obvious to me

00:12:39.622 --> 00:12:42.845
that people would take
the meaningful path.

00:12:42.845 --> 00:12:44.984
Y'know when you say, well
nihilists suffer dreadfully

00:12:44.984 --> 00:12:46.259
because there's no meaning in their life

00:12:46.259 --> 00:12:47.245
and they still suffer.

00:12:47.245 --> 00:12:50.439
Yeah, but the advantage is
they have no responsibility.

00:12:50.439 --> 00:12:51.628
So that's the payoff,

00:12:51.628 --> 00:12:53.259
and I actually think
that's the motivation.

00:12:53.259 --> 00:12:54.846
Say well I can't help being nihilistic.

00:12:54.846 --> 00:12:56.568
All my belief systems have collapsed.

00:12:56.568 --> 00:12:58.576
It's like, yeah maybe.

00:12:58.576 --> 00:12:59.833
Maybe you've just allowed them to collapse

00:12:59.833 --> 00:13:02.622
because it's a hell of a lot
easier than acting them out.

00:13:02.622 --> 00:13:04.538
And the price you pay is
some meaningless suffering.

00:13:04.538 --> 00:13:05.785
But you can always whine about that

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:07.318
and people will feel sorry for you.

00:13:07.318 --> 00:13:09.542
And you have the option of
taking the pathway of the martyr.

00:13:09.542 --> 00:13:12.101
So that's a pretty good
deal, all things considered.

00:13:12.101 --> 00:13:14.714
Especially, when the alternative is to

00:13:14.714 --> 00:13:16.881
bear your burden properly,

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.233
and to live forthrightly in the world.

00:13:20.233 --> 00:13:21.965
Well what Solzhenitsyn figured out,

00:13:21.965 --> 00:13:24.193
and so many people in
the twentieth century.

00:13:24.193 --> 00:13:26.741
It's not just him, even
though he's the best example,

00:13:26.741 --> 00:13:30.030
is that if you live a pathological life

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:32.584
you pathologize your society.

00:13:32.584 --> 00:13:36.326
And if enough people
do that then it's hell.

00:13:36.326 --> 00:13:37.576
Really, really.

00:13:39.792 --> 00:13:41.412
And you can read The Gulag Archipelago

00:13:41.412 --> 00:13:43.709
if you have the fortitude to do that

00:13:43.709 --> 00:13:45.924
and you will see exactly
what hell is like.

00:13:45.924 --> 00:13:46.775
And then you can decide

00:13:46.775 --> 00:13:49.096
if that's a place you'd like to visit.

00:13:49.096 --> 00:13:50.189
Or even more importantly,

00:13:50.189 --> 00:13:52.117
if its a place you'd like to visit

00:13:52.117 --> 00:13:55.256
and take all your family and friends.

00:13:55.256 --> 00:13:57.967
Because that's what happened
in the twentieth century.

00:13:57.967 --> 00:13:59.930
The next best predictor
of lifetime success

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:01.272
is conscientiousness.

00:14:01.272 --> 00:14:04.848
Of the two aspects of conscientiousness,

00:14:04.848 --> 00:14:08.338
say orderliness and industriousness,

00:14:08.338 --> 00:14:09.860
the better predictor is industriousness.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:11.908
So the question is, well
what can you do about

00:14:11.908 --> 00:14:13.126
your industriousness?

00:14:13.126 --> 00:14:16.559
And the answer to that is,
well that's kind of rough too,

00:14:16.559 --> 00:14:18.574
because there's a strong
genetic component.

00:14:18.574 --> 00:14:21.324
But, you can work on micro-habits

00:14:22.292 --> 00:14:23.956
with regards to your conscientiousness.

00:14:23.956 --> 00:14:25.555
And I think the best micro-habits,

00:14:25.555 --> 00:14:26.822
this is partly to do with

00:14:26.822 --> 00:14:29.003
these future offering program processes.

00:14:29.003 --> 00:14:30.234
I think the best thing you can do

00:14:30.234 --> 00:14:31.750
with regards to your conscientiousness,

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.372
is to set up some aims for yourself,

00:14:34.372 --> 00:14:36.359
goals that you actually value.

00:14:36.359 --> 00:14:39.686
The future offering program
helps people do that.

00:14:39.686 --> 00:14:42.944
Basically, it does a situational analysis.

00:14:42.944 --> 00:14:45.396
It helps you do a situational
analysis of your life,

00:14:45.396 --> 00:14:48.192
more than a psychological
analysis I would say.

00:14:48.192 --> 00:14:50.036
And so the questions are something like,

00:14:50.036 --> 00:14:52.355
well, all right, you're going
to have to put some effort

00:14:52.355 --> 00:14:53.775
into your life.

00:14:53.775 --> 00:14:56.369
You need to be motivated to do that.

00:14:56.369 --> 00:14:59.192
And so, what are the potential
sources of motivation?

00:14:59.192 --> 00:15:01.712
Well, you could think
about them in the big five.

00:15:01.712 --> 00:15:03.862
Manner, if you're
extroverted you want friends.

00:15:03.862 --> 00:15:05.691
If you're agreeable you want
an intimate relationship.

00:15:05.691 --> 00:15:08.494
If you're disagreeable you
want to win competitions.

00:15:08.494 --> 00:15:11.590
If you're open, you want to
engage in creative activity.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:14.676
If you're high in neuroticism
you want security.

00:15:14.676 --> 00:15:17.167
Okay, so those are all sources
of potential motivation,

00:15:17.167 --> 00:15:18.146
that you could draw on,

00:15:18.146 --> 00:15:21.274
that you could tailor
to your own personality.

00:15:21.274 --> 00:15:23.303
But then there are dimensions
that you want to consider

00:15:23.303 --> 00:15:24.307
your life across.

00:15:24.307 --> 00:15:26.195
And so we ask people about,

00:15:26.195 --> 00:15:28.679
well, if you could have the
life the way you wanted it

00:15:28.679 --> 00:15:29.635
in three to five years,

00:15:29.635 --> 00:15:31.910
if you were taking care
of yourself properly,

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:33.817
what would you want from your friendships?

00:15:33.817 --> 00:15:35.671
What would you want from
your intimate relationship?

00:15:35.671 --> 00:15:37.385
How would you like to
structure your family?

00:15:37.385 --> 00:15:39.308
What do you want for your career?

00:15:39.308 --> 00:15:42.523
How are you going to use your
time outside of your job?

00:15:42.523 --> 00:15:44.596
And how are you going to
regulate your mental, physical,

00:15:44.596 --> 00:15:46.059
mental and physical health,

00:15:46.059 --> 00:15:48.052
and maybe also your drug and alcohol use?

00:15:48.052 --> 00:15:50.289
Because that's a good place to auger down,

00:15:50.289 --> 00:15:52.058
'cuz alcohol, for example,

00:15:52.058 --> 00:15:54.200
wipes out 5 to 10 percent of people.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:57.283
So you want to keep that under control.

00:15:59.537 --> 00:16:02.736
And then, maybe you develop
a vision of what your life,

00:16:02.736 --> 00:16:04.278
what you would like your life to be,

00:16:04.278 --> 00:16:06.195
and that associates the

00:16:07.563 --> 00:16:09.760
so the goal, where once
the goal is established,

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.720
and then you break down the goal into

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.963
micro-processes that you can implement.

00:16:13.963 --> 00:16:17.729
The micro-processes become
rewarding, in proportion

00:16:17.729 --> 00:16:22.208
in relation to their causal
association with the goal.

00:16:22.208 --> 00:16:25.475
And that tangles in your
incentive reward system.

00:16:25.475 --> 00:16:26.907
We talked about the dopamine urge

00:16:26.907 --> 00:16:28.313
of consent and reward system,

00:16:28.313 --> 00:16:29.914
and that's the thing that
keeps you moving forward.

00:16:29.914 --> 00:16:32.441
And the way it works
is that it works better

00:16:32.441 --> 00:16:35.124
if it produces positive emotion

00:16:35.124 --> 00:16:38.187
when it can see you moving
towards a valued goal.

00:16:38.187 --> 00:16:40.561
Okay, well, what's the
implication of that?

00:16:40.561 --> 00:16:42.311
Better have a valued goal,

00:16:42.311 --> 00:16:44.634
because otherwise you can't
get any positive motivation

00:16:44.634 --> 00:16:47.182
working out, and so the
more valuable the goal,

00:16:47.182 --> 00:16:48.232
in principle,

00:16:48.232 --> 00:16:50.977
the more the micro-processes
associated with that goal

00:16:50.977 --> 00:16:53.638
start to take on a positive charge.

00:16:53.638 --> 00:16:54.806
And so what that means is,

00:16:54.806 --> 00:16:55.753
well you get up in the morning

00:16:55.753 --> 00:16:57.429
and you're excited about the day.

00:16:57.429 --> 00:16:58.300
You're ready to go.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:16:59.553
And so as far as I can tell,

00:16:59.553 --> 00:17:03.706
what you so is you specify
your long term ideal.

00:17:03.706 --> 00:17:05.397
Maybe you also specify a place you want to

00:17:05.397 --> 00:17:06.508
stay the hell away from,

00:17:06.508 --> 00:17:08.463
so that you're terrified to fail,

00:17:08.463 --> 00:17:10.336
as well as excited about succeeding,

00:17:10.336 --> 00:17:11.712
'cuz that's also useful.

00:17:11.712 --> 00:17:13.525
You specify your goal.

00:17:13.525 --> 00:17:17.654
You do that, in some sense
as a unique individual.

00:17:17.654 --> 00:17:20.324
You want to specify
goals that make you say,

00:17:20.324 --> 00:17:23.876
oh, if that could happen as
a consequence of my efforts,

00:17:23.876 --> 00:17:25.729
it would clearly be worthwhile.

00:17:25.729 --> 00:17:28.615
Because the question
always is why do something?

00:17:28.615 --> 00:17:29.967
'Cuz doing nothing is easy,

00:17:29.967 --> 00:17:31.806
you just sit there and
you don't do anything,

00:17:31.806 --> 00:17:32.775
that's real easy.

00:17:32.775 --> 00:17:35.096
The question is, why would
you ever do anything?

00:17:35.096 --> 00:17:36.187
And the answer to that has to be,

00:17:36.187 --> 00:17:38.700
because you've determined, by some means,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:39.975
that it's worthwhile.

00:17:39.975 --> 00:17:41.603
And then the next question might be,

00:17:41.603 --> 00:17:43.783
well where should you look
for worthwhile things?

00:17:43.783 --> 00:17:44.616
And one would be,

00:17:44.616 --> 00:17:46.506
well you could consult
your own temperament.

00:17:46.506 --> 00:17:49.563
The other would be, well
you kind of look at how,

00:17:49.563 --> 00:17:52.179
look at what it is that people accrue

00:17:52.179 --> 00:17:55.704
that's valuable across the lifespan.

00:17:55.704 --> 00:17:57.931
So you do a structural analysis of the

00:17:57.931 --> 00:18:00.286
subcomponents of human existence.

00:18:00.286 --> 00:18:01.776
Hardy did that.

00:18:01.776 --> 00:18:02.653
You need a family.

00:18:02.653 --> 00:18:03.848
You need friends.

00:18:03.848 --> 00:18:04.907
Like you don't need to
have all these things,

00:18:04.907 --> 00:18:06.239
but you better have most of them.

00:18:06.239 --> 00:18:09.099
Family, friends, career,
educational goals,

00:18:09.099 --> 00:18:12.187
plans for time outside of work.

00:18:12.187 --> 00:18:15.444
Attention to your mental and
physical health, et cetera.

00:18:15.444 --> 00:18:17.417
That's what life is about,

00:18:17.417 --> 00:18:19.836
and if you don't have any of those things,

00:18:19.836 --> 00:18:22.607
well, then all you've got
left is misery and suffering.

00:18:22.607 --> 00:18:24.696
So that's a bad,

00:18:24.696 --> 00:18:26.863
that's a bad deal for you.

00:18:29.373 --> 00:18:32.349
Once you've set up that
goal structure, let's say.

00:18:32.349 --> 00:18:34.974
And that's really, in many ways,

00:18:34.974 --> 00:18:36.778
that's what you should
be doing at university.

00:18:36.778 --> 00:18:38.286
That's exactly what you should be doing,

00:18:38.286 --> 00:18:39.873
is trying to figure out

00:18:39.873 --> 00:18:42.790
who it is that you're trying to be,

00:18:43.855 --> 00:18:45.721
and you aim at that.

00:18:45.721 --> 00:18:47.310
And then you use everything you learned,

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:50.287
as a means of building that
person that you want to be.

00:18:50.287 --> 00:18:52.764
And I really mean want to be.

00:18:52.764 --> 00:18:53.875
I don't mean should be,

00:18:53.875 --> 00:18:56.569
even though those things
are going to overlap.

00:18:56.569 --> 00:18:58.561
It's important to
distinguish between those,

00:18:58.561 --> 00:18:59.670
because that's partly,

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:01.886
and this is back down to
the micro-routine analysis,

00:19:01.886 --> 00:19:02.816
so it's like saying

00:19:02.816 --> 00:19:04.452
you're going to try to make
yourself more industrious,

00:19:04.452 --> 00:19:06.799
okay number one, specify your damn goals.

00:19:06.799 --> 00:19:07.936
'Cuz how are you going to hit something,

00:19:07.936 --> 00:19:09.210
if you don't know what it is?

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:10.799
That isn't going to happen.

00:19:10.799 --> 00:19:12.564
Often people won't
specify their goals too,

00:19:12.564 --> 00:19:15.421
because they don't like to
specify conditions for failure.

00:19:15.421 --> 00:19:17.372
So if you keep yourself
all vague and foggy,

00:19:17.372 --> 00:19:19.063
which is real easy,

00:19:19.063 --> 00:19:21.062
'cuz that's just a matter
of not doing, as well.

00:19:21.062 --> 00:19:23.129
Then you don't know when you fail.

00:19:23.129 --> 00:19:23.962
And people might say,

00:19:23.962 --> 00:19:25.596
well I really don't
want to know when I fail

00:19:25.596 --> 00:19:26.844
because that's painful.

00:19:26.844 --> 00:19:29.679
So I'll keep myself
blind about when I fail.

00:19:29.679 --> 00:19:32.066
That's fine except, you'll
fail all the time then.

00:19:32.066 --> 00:19:34.303
You just won't know it
until you've failed so badly

00:19:34.303 --> 00:19:35.905
that you're done.

00:19:35.905 --> 00:19:38.532
And that can easily by the time you're 40.

00:19:38.532 --> 00:19:42.421
So, I would recommend that
you don't let that happen.

00:19:42.421 --> 00:19:44.278
So that's willful blindness, right.

00:19:44.278 --> 00:19:46.669
You could've known, but you chose not to.

00:19:46.669 --> 00:19:49.034
Okay, so once you get
your goal structure set up

00:19:49.034 --> 00:19:51.417
you think, okay if I could have this life,

00:19:51.417 --> 00:19:52.943
looks like that might be worth living.

00:19:52.943 --> 00:19:54.573
Despite the fact that it's going to be

00:19:54.573 --> 00:19:56.943
anxiety provoking and threatening,

00:19:56.943 --> 00:19:58.868
and there's going to be some
suffering and loss involved

00:19:58.868 --> 00:20:00.566
and all of that, obviously.

00:20:00.566 --> 00:20:03.096
The goal is to have a
vision for your life,

00:20:03.096 --> 00:20:06.355
such that, all things considered,

00:20:06.355 --> 00:20:08.449
that justifies your effort.

00:20:08.449 --> 00:20:10.636
I started to pay very careful attention to

00:20:10.636 --> 00:20:12.136
what I was saying.

00:20:13.202 --> 00:20:15.392
I don't know if that happened
voluntarily or involuntarily,

00:20:15.392 --> 00:20:19.051
but I could feel a sort of
split developing in my psyche.

00:20:19.051 --> 00:20:20.811
This split, and I've
actually had students tell me

00:20:20.811 --> 00:20:22.932
the same thing has happened to them

00:20:22.932 --> 00:20:25.965
after they listened to
some of the material

00:20:25.965 --> 00:20:29.298
that I've been describing to all of you.

00:20:30.443 --> 00:20:32.776
I split into two, let's say,

00:20:34.225 --> 00:20:37.226
and one part was the, let's say the old me

00:20:37.226 --> 00:20:39.326
that was talking a lot,

00:20:39.326 --> 00:20:41.337
and that liked to argue,

00:20:41.337 --> 00:20:43.252
and that liked ideas.

00:20:43.252 --> 00:20:46.562
And there was another part
that was watching that part

00:20:46.562 --> 00:20:48.434
just with its eyes open,

00:20:48.434 --> 00:20:50.845
and neutrally judging.

00:20:50.845 --> 00:20:53.122
And the part that was neutrally judging

00:20:53.122 --> 00:20:54.690
was watching the part that was talking

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.924
and going, that's isn't your idea.

00:20:57.924 --> 00:20:59.925
You don't really believe that.

00:20:59.925 --> 00:21:02.101
You don't really know
what you're talking about.

00:21:02.101 --> 00:21:03.626
That isn't true.

00:21:03.626 --> 00:21:06.102
I thought, hmm, that's really interesting.

00:21:06.102 --> 00:21:09.433
That was happening to like 95
percent of what I was saying.

00:21:09.433 --> 00:21:10.828
So then I didn't really know what to do.

00:21:10.828 --> 00:21:12.301
I thought, okay this is strange.

00:21:12.301 --> 00:21:14.363
So maybe, I've fragmented,

00:21:14.363 --> 00:21:16.173
and that's just not a good thing at all.

00:21:16.173 --> 00:21:18.636
I mean it wasn't like I was
hearing voices or anything

00:21:18.636 --> 00:21:21.678
like that, it wasn't like that.

00:21:21.678 --> 00:21:24.928
It was well people have multiple parts.

00:21:25.966 --> 00:21:27.823
So then I had this weird conundrum,

00:21:27.823 --> 00:21:31.400
it was like, well which of
these two things are me?

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.698
Is it the part that's
listening and saying,

00:21:33.698 --> 00:21:37.294
no that's rubbish, that's a lie, that's

00:21:37.294 --> 00:21:39.166
you're doing that to impress people,

00:21:39.166 --> 00:21:41.922
you're just trying to
win the argument, y'know.

00:21:41.922 --> 00:21:44.155
Was that me? Or was the
part that was going about

00:21:44.155 --> 00:21:47.012
my normal verbal business me?

00:21:47.012 --> 00:21:49.872
And I didn't know, but I decided
I would go with the critic.

00:21:49.872 --> 00:21:52.742
And then what I tried to
do, what I learned to do,

00:21:52.742 --> 00:21:56.909
I think, was to stop saying
things that made me weak.

00:21:58.203 --> 00:22:00.523
I mean I'm still trying to do that,

00:22:00.523 --> 00:22:03.369
because I'm always feeling when I talk

00:22:03.369 --> 00:22:06.038
whether or not the words that I'm saying

00:22:06.038 --> 00:22:07.772
are either making me align

00:22:07.772 --> 00:22:10.216
or making me come apart.

00:22:10.216 --> 00:22:12.843
I think the alignment, I
really do think the alignment,

00:22:12.843 --> 00:22:15.269
I think alignment is the right
way of conceptualizing it,

00:22:15.269 --> 00:22:17.339
because I think if you say things that are

00:22:17.339 --> 00:22:20.933
as true as you can say them, let's say,

00:22:20.933 --> 00:22:22.433
then they come up,

00:22:23.395 --> 00:22:25.849
they come out of the depths inside of you.

00:22:25.849 --> 00:22:27.471
We don't know where thoughts come from.

00:22:27.471 --> 00:22:29.762
We don't know how far down
into your substructure

00:22:29.762 --> 00:22:31.341
the thoughts emerge.

00:22:31.341 --> 00:22:34.237
We don't know what processes
of physiological alignment

00:22:34.237 --> 00:22:37.952
are necessary for you to speak
from the core of your being.

00:22:37.952 --> 00:22:39.112
We don't understand any of that.

00:22:39.112 --> 00:22:40.387
We don't even conceptualize that.

00:22:40.387 --> 00:22:41.814
But I believe that you can feel that.

00:22:41.814 --> 00:22:43.951
I learned some of that
from reading Carl Rogers,

00:22:43.951 --> 00:22:46.168
by the way, who's a great clinician.

00:22:46.168 --> 00:22:48.536
Because he talked about
mental health, in part,

00:22:48.536 --> 00:22:53.332
as a coherence between the
spiritual or the abstract

00:22:53.332 --> 00:22:54.457
and the physical.

00:22:54.457 --> 00:22:56.396
That the two things were aligned.

00:22:56.396 --> 00:22:58.387
There's a lot of idea of alignment

00:22:58.387 --> 00:23:01.185
in psychoanalytic and clinical thinking.

00:23:01.185 --> 00:23:04.588
But, anyways, I decided that
I would start practicing

00:23:04.588 --> 00:23:07.196
not saying things that would make me weak.

00:23:07.196 --> 00:23:09.856
What happened was that
I had to stop saying

00:23:09.856 --> 00:23:12.615
almost everything that I was saying.

00:23:12.615 --> 00:23:14.414
I would say 95 percent of it.

00:23:14.414 --> 00:23:16.017
That's a hell of a shock to wake up,

00:23:16.017 --> 00:23:18.025
I mean, this was over a few months,

00:23:18.025 --> 00:23:20.767
but it's a hell of a shock
to wake up and realize that

00:23:20.767 --> 00:23:22.767
you're mostly dead wood.

00:23:23.834 --> 00:23:25.340
It's a shock.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.453
And you might think,

00:23:26.453 --> 00:23:28.502
well do you really want
all of that to burn off?

00:23:28.502 --> 00:23:30.280
There's nothing left but a little husk

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:31.311
five percent of you.

00:23:31.311 --> 00:23:35.263
It's like, well, if that
five percent is solid,

00:23:35.263 --> 00:23:38.674
then maybe that's exactly
what you want to have happen.

00:23:38.674 --> 00:23:41.507
Adopt the mode of authentic being.

00:23:44.364 --> 00:23:45.993
And that is something like,

00:23:45.993 --> 00:23:49.603
refusing to participate in the lie,

00:23:49.603 --> 00:23:51.044
in deception and the lie.

00:23:51.044 --> 00:23:53.388
To orient your speech as much as you can

00:23:53.388 --> 00:23:54.888
towards the truth.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:58.096
And to take responsibility
for your own life,

00:23:58.096 --> 00:24:00.399
and perhaps for the lives of other people.

00:24:00.399 --> 00:24:02.695
And there's something about that that's

00:24:02.695 --> 00:24:05.787
meaningful, and responsible, and noble,

00:24:05.787 --> 00:24:09.938
but also serves to mitigate the
very suffering that produces

00:24:09.938 --> 00:24:13.771
say the nihilism, or the
fleeing into the arms

00:24:15.278 --> 00:24:19.293
or the escape into the arms of
totalitarians to begin with.

00:24:19.293 --> 00:24:21.365
You need something to shelter you against

00:24:21.365 --> 00:24:23.235
your own vulnerability.

00:24:23.235 --> 00:24:25.574
You can think about the world this way.

00:24:25.574 --> 00:24:28.269
You can think about it as
your orderly little plan.

00:24:28.269 --> 00:24:29.170
That's a place.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:30.555
And you can think about it as the place

00:24:30.555 --> 00:24:33.278
that things that disrupt
your plan comes from.

00:24:33.278 --> 00:24:34.225
That's another place.

00:24:34.225 --> 00:24:37.167
This is a bigger place than this.

00:24:37.167 --> 00:24:39.311
Because there's an
endless number of things

00:24:39.311 --> 00:24:40.547
that can disrupt your plan,

00:24:40.547 --> 00:24:41.855
and only a tiny number of them

00:24:41.855 --> 00:24:44.694
that will help you work it out.

00:24:44.694 --> 00:24:46.196
So, part of the question then too,

00:24:46.196 --> 00:24:48.706
is like, are you the friend of your plan,

00:24:48.706 --> 00:24:51.639
or are you the friend of the
thing that disrupts your plan?

00:24:51.639 --> 00:24:54.024
I would say, you should
work to become the friend

00:24:54.024 --> 00:24:55.667
of the the thing that disrupts your plan,

00:24:55.667 --> 00:24:58.178
because there's a lot of that.

00:24:58.178 --> 00:25:00.149
And then if you become
the friend of the thing

00:25:00.149 --> 00:25:01.528
that disrupts your plan,

00:25:01.528 --> 00:25:04.097
then you start to develop strength

00:25:04.097 --> 00:25:07.005
in proportion to the disruptive force,

00:25:07.005 --> 00:25:08.236
and that's really what you want.

00:25:08.236 --> 00:25:09.917
You want to be able to
implement your plan, obviously.

00:25:09.917 --> 00:25:12.535
But you want to be able to take on

00:25:12.535 --> 00:25:16.369
the consequences of
error and learn from it,

00:25:16.369 --> 00:25:18.337
and then you win constantly.

00:25:18.337 --> 00:25:19.742
Because even if something goes sideways,

00:25:19.742 --> 00:25:23.510
you think there's something
to be derived from this.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:25.495
That's wisdom, fundamentally.

00:25:25.495 --> 00:25:28.017
Plan a life you'd like to have,

00:25:28.017 --> 00:25:32.085
and you do that partly by
referring to social norms.

00:25:32.085 --> 00:25:33.783
That's more or less rescuing your father

00:25:33.783 --> 00:25:35.074
from the belly of the whale.

00:25:35.074 --> 00:25:37.107
But the other way you do that is by

00:25:37.107 --> 00:25:39.155
having a little
conversation with yourself,

00:25:39.155 --> 00:25:41.540
as if you don't really know who you are

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:42.887
because you know what you're like.

00:25:42.887 --> 00:25:44.225
You won't do what you're told.

00:25:44.225 --> 00:25:46.624
You won't do what you tell yourself to do.

00:25:46.624 --> 00:25:48.109
You must have noticed that.

00:25:48.109 --> 00:25:51.802
It's like you're a bad
employee, and a worse boss.

00:25:51.802 --> 00:25:54.507
Both of those work for you.

00:25:54.507 --> 00:25:55.582
You don't know what you want to do,

00:25:55.582 --> 00:25:56.908
and then when you tell
yourself what to do,

00:25:56.908 --> 00:25:57.838
you don't do it anyway.

00:25:57.838 --> 00:26:00.630
So you should fire yourself,
and find someone else to be.

00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:04.843
But, my point is that
you have to understand

00:26:04.843 --> 00:26:07.018
that you're not your own
servant, so to speak.

00:26:07.018 --> 00:26:09.886
You're someone that you
have to negotiate with.

00:26:09.886 --> 00:26:12.530
You're someone that you want to present

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:15.146
the opportunity of having a good life to.

00:26:15.146 --> 00:26:16.066
That's hard for people,

00:26:16.066 --> 00:26:18.522
'cuz they don't like themselves very much.

00:26:18.522 --> 00:26:20.715
So, they're always like cracking the whip

00:26:20.715 --> 00:26:22.116
and then procrastinating,

00:26:22.116 --> 00:26:22.996
and cracking the whip

00:26:22.996 --> 00:26:24.123
and then procrastinating.

00:26:24.123 --> 00:26:26.156
And it's like god, it's so boring,

00:26:26.156 --> 00:26:29.115
and it's such a pathetic
way of spending your time.

00:26:29.115 --> 00:26:31.341
If you can figure your life,

00:26:31.341 --> 00:26:33.591
so that what you are generally doing

00:26:33.591 --> 00:26:37.592
is aiming at the highest possible good,

00:26:37.592 --> 00:26:40.925
then the things that you need to survive

00:26:42.912 --> 00:26:45.282
and to thrive on a day to day basis

00:26:45.282 --> 00:26:47.865
will deliver themselves to you.

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:50.269
That's a hypothesis,

00:26:50.269 --> 00:26:52.393
and it's not some simple
hypothesis, right?

00:26:52.393 --> 00:26:54.541
Because what it basically says is,

00:26:54.541 --> 00:26:56.100
if you dare to do the most difficult thing

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.010
that you can conceptualize

00:26:58.010 --> 00:26:59.448
your life will work out better

00:26:59.448 --> 00:27:02.203
than it will if you do anything else.

00:27:02.203 --> 00:27:04.222
Well how are you going to
find out if that's true?

00:27:04.222 --> 00:27:06.076
It's a Kierkegaardian leap of faith.

00:27:06.076 --> 00:27:07.637
There's no way you're going to find out

00:27:07.637 --> 00:27:09.864
whether or not that's
true unless you do it.

00:27:09.864 --> 00:27:11.935
No one can tell you either,

00:27:11.935 --> 00:27:14.743
because just because it
works for someone else,

00:27:14.743 --> 00:27:16.900
I mean that's interesting and all that.

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:18.648
But it's no proof that it'll work for you.

00:27:18.648 --> 00:27:20.730
You have to be all in, in this game.

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:23.567
There is no more effective
way of operating in the world,

00:27:23.567 --> 00:27:26.989
then to conceptualize the
highest good that you can,

00:27:26.989 --> 00:27:29.016
and then strive to to attain it.

00:27:29.016 --> 00:27:33.183
There's no more practical
pathway to the kind of success

00:27:34.743 --> 00:27:38.589
that you could have if you
actually knew what success was.

00:27:38.589 --> 00:27:41.922
The world shifts itself around your aim.

00:27:42.859 --> 00:27:44.938
You're a creature that has an aim.

00:27:44.938 --> 00:27:46.984
You have to have an aim
in order to do something.

00:27:46.984 --> 00:27:48.255
You're an aiming creature.

00:27:48.255 --> 00:27:51.185
You look at a point and
you move towards it.

00:27:51.185 --> 00:27:53.186
It's built right into you.

00:27:53.186 --> 00:27:54.253
And so you have an aim.

00:27:54.253 --> 00:27:56.797
Well, let's say your aim is
the highest possible aim.

00:27:56.797 --> 00:28:00.192
Well, then, that sets
up the world around you.

00:28:00.192 --> 00:28:02.227
It organizes all of your perceptions.

00:28:02.227 --> 00:28:04.360
It organizes what you
see and you don't see.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:07.600
It organizes your emotions
and your motivations.

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.311
So you organize yourself around that aim.

00:28:09.311 --> 00:28:12.144
And then what happens is
the day manifests itself

00:28:12.144 --> 00:28:14.612
as a set of challenges and problems.

00:28:14.612 --> 00:28:17.003
If you solve them properly,

00:28:17.003 --> 00:28:19.352
then you stay on the
pathway towards that aim.

00:28:19.352 --> 00:28:22.069
And you can concentrate on the day,

00:28:22.069 --> 00:28:24.451
and so that way you get to
have your cake and eat it too,

00:28:24.451 --> 00:28:27.432
because you can point into the distance,

00:28:27.432 --> 00:28:29.049
the far distance,

00:28:29.049 --> 00:28:31.382
and you can live in the day.

00:28:32.574 --> 00:28:36.160
It seems to me, that makes
every moment of the day

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.932
supercharged with meaning,

00:28:37.932 --> 00:28:40.956
If everything that you're doing every day,

00:28:40.956 --> 00:28:43.250
is related to the highest possible aim

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:44.786
that you can conceptualize,

00:28:44.786 --> 00:28:46.713
well that's the very
definition of the meaning

00:28:46.713 --> 00:28:48.278
that would sustain you in you life.

00:28:48.278 --> 00:28:51.091
(swish)

00:28:51.091 --> 00:28:52.877
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:28:52.877 --> 00:28:55.828
I made this video because
dudewat two one two

00:28:55.828 --> 00:28:56.974
asked me to.

00:28:56.974 --> 00:28:57.987
So if there is a famous entrepreneur

00:28:57.987 --> 00:28:59.314
that you want me to profile next

00:28:59.314 --> 00:29:00.842
check out the link
that's in the description

00:29:00.842 --> 00:29:02.848
and you can go and cast your vote.

00:29:02.848 --> 00:29:04.150
I'd also love to know which clip

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:05.496
resonated the most with you?

00:29:05.496 --> 00:29:07.389
What lesson are you going
to take from this video

00:29:07.389 --> 00:29:09.390
and immediately apply to your life

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:10.689
or to your business somehow.

00:29:10.689 --> 00:29:12.283
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:29:12.283 --> 00:29:14.498
I'm really curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:29:14.498 --> 00:29:16.397
I also want to give a
quick shout out to Nathan

00:29:16.397 --> 00:29:18.415
from the Productivity Game Channel.

00:29:18.415 --> 00:29:20.711
Nathan, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:29:20.711 --> 00:29:23.144
Your One Word, and making
that awesome YouTube video

00:29:23.144 --> 00:29:24.622
on your channel about it.

00:29:24.622 --> 00:29:26.316
I really appreciate the support man.

00:29:26.316 --> 00:29:28.243
And I'm so glad you enjoyed my book.

00:29:28.243 --> 00:29:30.043
- [Nathan] Carmichael states
that there is one word

00:29:30.043 --> 00:29:32.216
that defines who you are,

00:29:32.216 --> 00:29:34.140
connects all the things in your life,

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:35.513
that make you come alive,

00:29:35.513 --> 00:29:38.310
and will help you escape
the chains of mediocrity.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:39.544
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:29:39.544 --> 00:29:40.961
I believe in you.

00:29:40.961 --> 00:29:42.574
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:29:42.574 --> 00:29:45.189
and whatever your one word is,

00:29:45.189 --> 00:29:46.722
much love.

00:29:46.722 --> 00:29:48.345
I'll see you soon.

00:29:48.345 --> 00:29:50.345
